1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,720
Hundreds of thousands of EU gamers
have signed a petition.

2
00:00:03,826 --> 00:00:06,580
They condemn the practice of
some game publishers

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,580
who deactivate their
online servers

4
00:00:08,626 --> 00:00:12,980
rendering old games
unusable overnight.

5
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:16,560
The shutdown of The Crew by
french publisher Ubisoft on April 1st

6
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,980
has sparked things off, explanations by
Jean-Baptiste Sarrazin & Mathis Godefroy

7
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:26,960
Since April, when this content creator
inserts this racing game in his console

8
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:28,820
the situation remains the same.

9
00:00:28,853 --> 00:00:33,306
We cannot access this game at all,
not even the solo story mode

10
00:00:33,573 --> 00:00:38,680
Released in 2014, this game published by
Ubisoft has since had 2 sequels.

11
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,946
But many gamers like him are rising up
against the removal of online servers.

12
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:46,520
In cases like The Crew, it means
the loss of the multiplayer parts

13
00:00:46,613 --> 00:00:48,013
as well as the solo parts.

14
00:00:48,146 --> 00:00:51,200
Battleborn, which is a game
released in 2016 I think,

15
00:00:51,306 --> 00:00:55,080
in 2021 they stopped the servers,
and the worst here is that

16
00:00:55,186 --> 00:01:00,320
there are people who kept buying it,
even though the game was completely unusable.

17
00:01:00,466 --> 00:01:03,040
In order to try to combat those practices,

18
00:01:03,173 --> 00:01:07,333
a european citizen's initiative petition
was launched by a slovak gamer.

19
00:01:07,453 --> 00:01:11,160
But Axel has decided to support
the cause on a national level.

20
00:01:11,493 --> 00:01:16,013
Thank you for being there, it's a pleasure.

21
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:19,986
To do that, he surrounded himself with
peers, but also jurists and academics.

22
00:01:20,066 --> 00:01:23,400
The goal: to find solutions which,
for them, are obvious.

23
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:26,466
To be able to play offline,
that's what we want.

24
00:01:26,586 --> 00:01:30,213
We simply want to preserve
video games, culture,

25
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:33,613
it's embarassing, we have works
that completely disappear.

26
00:01:33,786 --> 00:01:37,780
But according to this expert,
publishers are complying with the law.

27
00:01:37,893 --> 00:01:42,106
Video games are intellectual works,
protected by copyright.

28
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:45,813
The publisher has certain prerogatives,
due to that copyright,

29
00:01:45,893 --> 00:01:48,346
in particular the "right of withdrawal".

30
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:51,306
As consumers, gamers want to
assert their rights

31
00:01:51,373 --> 00:01:53,813
and oppose the publishers
commercial practices.

32
00:01:54,106 --> 00:01:57,093
Ubisoft didn't want to comment.

33
00:01:57,186 --> 00:02:00,706
If the european petition gets
1 million signatures by next July,

34
00:02:00,786 --> 00:02:04,320
EU executives will have to
provide an answer.

